# Built-in Emacs Lisp to remove from the installed copy (paths relative to
# emacs-src/lisp/). A trailing "/" means a whole directory; other lines are
# globs. prune.py rescues any entry that a kept file still requires.

# Games and toys
play/

# Mail and news readers (shared libs like rfc822, mail-utils, sendmail stay)
gnus/
mh-e/
mail/rmail*.el
mail/undigest.el
mail/unrmail.el
mail/feedmail.el
mail/mspools.el
mail/supercite.el

# Chat
erc/
net/rcirc.el

# Legacy IDE tooling (CEDET, EDE, Semantic, SRecode)
cedet/

# Exceptions ("!" = never remove). Found by tracing real workflows, not by the
# dependency scan, because they are loaded lazily at run time:
# url -> mm-decode loads this when package.el downloads anything.
!gnus/mm-archive.el
# tablist (a real, hard dependency of the docker/kubernetes packages) unconditionally
# requires this at its own top level for its "/" filter-expression feature (confirmed
# directly: config/elpa/tablist-*/tablist.el -> tablist-filter.el -> (require
# 'semantic/wisent/comp) -- a real "Cannot open load file" error once cedet/ above is
# pruned, not assumed). prune.py's own rescue pass (its needs()/plan() only scans
# emacs-src/lisp for dependencies BETWEEN built-in files, never config/elpa) has no way
# to know a third-party package needs this, so this gap only surfaces when a package
# that actually needs it gets installed. Only this ONE file needed adding: tablist's
# other require, `semantic/wisent/wisent', is already rescued on its own (confirmed in
# `prune.py --plan`'s own output), via a completely unrelated chain --- org's own
# Python Babel support (`org/ob-python') already needs CEDET's Python parser, which
# pulls in the wisent parser *runtime*; only the wisent grammar *compiler* (`comp.el',
# used for `tablist-filter''s on-the-fly filter-expression grammar) was still missing.
!cedet/semantic/wisent/comp.el
